From e4f67b1ea29a7108b8cf709b575fc7fadfd5ec76 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: olympia <olympia@xbmc.org>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 21:57:09 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] FFmpegImage: avoid an unwritten last column in odd-width YUV
 conversion

FFmpeg's unscaled YUV-to-RGB C converter (yuv2rgb_c_32 and yuva2rgba_c
in libswscale/yuv2rgb.c) writes 8, 4 and 2 pixel blocks and never the
final pixel of an odd width. swscale picks it for unscaled YUV420P,
YUV422P and YUVA420P to RGB conversions when the height is even and
SWS_ACCURATE_RND is not set; the width is not checked. The ARM NEON
replacement needs a width divisible by 16, so on armhf the C converter
runs. Kodi decodes into an uncleared texture buffer, so the last image
column held leftover memory, and ClampToEdge() copied it into the
padding. That gave the changing line on the right edge of some posters.

For exactly that case request SWS_ACCURATE_RND, which makes swscale
take its generic path. It writes every column and keeps the image size
and the full range handling. Clearing the buffer would only have hidden
the edge. Other conversions keep SWS_BICUBIC. Accurate rounding may
change RGB values slightly against the fast path.

Checked against FFmpeg n6.0.1, which Kodi 21.3 builds. FFmpeg master
(aab6cec) still has the same block structure and selection.

Validated on the Vero V with the 379x540 Moonraker cache JPEG: eight
separate loads showed no artifact and gave byte-identical upload
buffers.
---
 xbmc/guilib/FFmpegImage.cpp | 13 ++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/xbmc/guilib/FFmpegImage.cpp b/xbmc/guilib/FFmpegImage.cpp
index 2cf3eb4..af0c6ba 100644
--- a/xbmc/guilib/FFmpegImage.cpp
+++ b/xbmc/guilib/FFmpegImage.cpp
@@ -505,8 +505,19 @@ bool CFFmpegImage::DecodeFrame(AVFrame* frame, unsigned int width, unsigned int
   nWidth = std::max(nWidth, 1u);
   nHeight = std::max(nHeight, 1u);
 
+  int swsFlags = SWS_BICUBIC;
+  // FFmpeg's unscaled YUV-to-RGB C path leaves the last column of an odd width unwritten;
+  // accurate rounding makes swscale take its generic path instead.
+  const bool oddUnscaledYuv =
+      (nWidth & 1) != 0 && (nHeight & 1) == 0 &&
+      nWidth == m_originalWidth && nHeight == m_originalHeight &&
+      (pixFormat == AV_PIX_FMT_YUV420P || pixFormat == AV_PIX_FMT_YUV422P ||
+       pixFormat == AV_PIX_FMT_YUVA420P);
+  if (oddUnscaledYuv)
+    swsFlags |= SWS_ACCURATE_RND;
+
   struct SwsContext* context = sws_getContext(m_originalWidth, m_originalHeight, pixFormat,
-    nWidth, nHeight, AV_PIX_FMT_RGB32, SWS_BICUBIC, NULL, NULL, NULL);
+    nWidth, nHeight, AV_PIX_FMT_RGB32, swsFlags, NULL, NULL, NULL);
   if (!context)
   {
     CLog::LogF(LOGERROR, "Could not create a scaling context for {} x {} pixels", nWidth,
